notes:
Maintainer risk: MEDIUM — the principal_id seam and CallerCtx threading are well-shaped and documented; the SetError.extra and DoS mitigations are sound choices; but two real footguns remain (silent merge-patch truncation as a data-integrity bug, handler-side leak of backend Display contract). Fixing JMAP-wlip.1 and JMAP-wlip.2 brings risk to LOW.
Confidence: ~15% chance of missed bug. Least confident flags: JMAP-wlip.7 (createdIds collision could be intentional last-write-wins; pre-existing); JMAP-wlip.6 (principal_id no-&self might be deliberate workspace-level constraint already accepted by future consumers). Uncertainty driven by: (a) cannot see runtime behaviour of all five extension *-server crates downstream of the JmapBackend trait reshape — there may be canonical-propagation work tracked elsewhere that already addresses some of these (e.g. handler-side Display redaction may exist or be planned in jmap-mail-server); (b) did not exercise the criterion benches to check whether the per-iter clone overhead actually swamps the parse_request work (could be a noisy bench but still useful directionally); (c) no integration tests for the full HTTP→dispatch→backend round trip means TOCTOU-style state-vs-data drift between get_objects/get_state in handlers.rs cannot be observed in this review. Would raise confidence if: ran cargo test --workspace across all sibling *-server crates to confirm CallerCtx threading works end-to-end; could see the operator-log shape consumers wire on top of RequestError::Display to confirm the leak path in JMAP-wlip.2; had a worked example of a real chat /set update PatchObject exceeding 32 levels to confirm the legitimate-input claim in MAX_MERGE_PATCH_DEPTH.
API contract risk: MEDIUM — 10 findings across all 6 sub-categories (immutability, underspecified, versioning, leakage, defensive, docs). No P0/P1 contract bugs but multiple P2 holes that bite real consumers: ClosureHandler pub-fields lock the shape (.17), Custom-vs-named SetErrorType round-trip asymmetry (.22), unworkable per-request cancellation pattern (.23), and SetError.with_description doc-leak gap that parallels JMAP-wlip.2 (.26). Distribution: 5 P2 (immutability x1, leakage x1, underspecified x2, docs x1), 5 P3 (versioning x1, leakage x1, docs x1, defensive x2). Closing all 10 brings risk to LOW.
API contract confidence: ~20% chance of missed issue. Least confident flags: JMAP-wlip.21 (ser-rename — clarity-cost argument is real but borderline opinion-adjacent; some reviewers may classify as taste); JMAP-wlip.23 (cancellation — diagnosis is right but recommended CancellationToken plumbing is speculative; workspace may have a planned alternative path I cannot see). Uncertainty driven by: (a) did not probe panic isolation under synchronous-handler-construction (panic before await) — documented but unverified; (b) did not measure whether jmap-types re-exports are deliberate one-stop-shopping convenience or accidental (PLAN.md does not say); (c) did not check downstream consumers outside this workspace (kith, stoa) for shape-locked patterns that would make some findings non-actionable in the near term; (d) JMAP-wlip.19 (mail variants in foundation SetErrorType) is pre-existing in the baseline 9c0d34f — surfacing it now is valid contract feedback but it may have been a deliberate workspace decision predating this review window that I'm not party to. Would raise confidence if: ran a probe asserting SetErrorType::custom('forbidden') round-trips back to Custom (it doesn't, but confirming the negative is worth a 5-line test); could see the workspace's planned major-bump roadmap for whether mail-variant relocation is on the list; had access to consumer crates (kith, stoa) to check which of these contract holes already bit them.
Idiom rating: ACCEPTABLE — the code is competently idiomatic; 6 real findings (one P1 cast safety, two P2 maintenance burdens, two P3 type-design / DRY, one P4 attribute redundancy). No unsafe, no Box<dyn Error> in library API, no .unwrap()/.expect() outside tests except documented-infallible call sites, no exported macro_rules!, lifetime annotations elide correctly. The non-idiomatic patterns are mostly forced by constraints documented in workspace AGENTS.md (foundation-type Invocation tuple shape from RFC 8620 wire format; manual SetErrorType serde impl forced by 5-crate dep policy; Box::pin closure boilerplate forced by stable-Rust async-fn-in-trait constraints). Fixing JMAP-wlip.27 (try_from casts) is the only clear-win delta; the rest are workspace-architecture discussions surfaced as beads.
Rust idiom confidence: ~25% chance of missed issue. Least confident flags: JMAP-wlip.29 (SetErrorType manual serde — workspace dep policy may have settled this; the deps-restriction in crate AGENTS.md is explicit), JMAP-wlip.30 (Invocation tuple — foundation-type shape; may be intentional to match JMAP wire serialization shape verbatim and minimize Value materialization). Uncertainty driven by: (a) did not benchmark the iter().map().collect() vs direct Vec<Id> in json!() to confirm the wasted-allocation claim in .28 actually shows up; (b) the 'as' casts in helpers.rs are bounded by algorithm in practice so try_from would only change panic-site-location, not behaviour on legitimate input; (c) did not survey other workspace crates to see if the .0/.1/.2 Invocation indexing is a pattern across all *-server crates or just jmap-server; (d) did not check whether strum-style dep allowlist exemption is on any roadmap to remove the manual SetErrorType serde burden. Would raise confidence if: ran cargo bench to confirm the json! allocation cost is real; could see the original kith Invocation type-design rationale; had a worked example of a SetErrorType variant being added to confirm the Display/Deserialize sync burden in practice.
